// core/filesystem is the small hosted directory-enumeration boundary used by
// interactive tools. Its input is one borrowed, zero-terminated UTF-8 path;
// its output is an explicitly owned Directory handle plus one copied entry
// name at a time. The caller owns all name storage and must close every
// successfully opened Directory exactly once.
//
// Enumeration order is deliberately unspecified because native filesystems do
// not provide a semantic order. A caller whose result is user-visible or enters
// a manifest/hash must sort the copied names explicitly. Target-selected files
// own native handle/layout/encoding details. This common file owns only the
// portable value vocabulary. Draft specification: sections 3, 5-7, and 12,
// plus the selected target profile.
package filesystem

import core/io

// Entry_Kind is a best-effort native classification. `unknown` includes
// symlinks and filesystem objects whose type the enumeration API does not
// expose. A caller that wants to enter an `other` row as a directory may try
// opening that complete path; classification is not an authorization check.
pub Entry_Kind :: enum u8 {
    unknown,
    regular_file,
    directory,
}

// Entry describes the prefix initialized in the destination passed to read.
// name_length is a byte count, excludes any terminator, and is valid only when
// read returns io.Error.none. Names are UTF-8 on every hosted target.
pub Entry :: struct {
    kind: Entry_Kind,
    name_length: usize,
}
